OpenAmpere

Register definitions for FoxESS inverters are based on facts from foxess_modbus
(https://github.com/nathanmarlor/foxess_modbus), Copyright (c) 2021 nathanmarlor, MIT License.

Register definitions for SAJ H2/HS2 inverters are based on facts from
home-assistant-saj-h2-modbus (https://github.com/stanus74/home-assistant-saj-h2-modbus, MIT License),
home-assistant-ampere-storage-pro-modbus (https://github.com/dboeni/home-assistant-ampere-storage-pro-modbus,
MIT License), EKD_Telegraf_Modbus (https://github.com/litronics/EKD_Telegraf_Modbus, Apache-2.0) and the
evcc saj-h2 template (https://github.com/evcc-io/evcc, MIT License). The control of SAJ inverters (battery
settings, export limit, charge slots) follows SAJ's H2 communication protocol, shipped with
home-assistant-saj-h2-modbus, and the way that project and evcc write these registers.

Register definitions for Solarmax MAX.STORAGE inverters are based on facts from Solarmax's "MAX.STORAGE /
MAX.STORAGE Ultimate ModbusTCP Datenpunktliste" (2024-09-11), checked against the evcc solarmax-maxstorage template
(https://github.com/evcc-io/evcc, MIT License).

Wallboxes are controlled by evcc (https://evcc.io, https://github.com/evcc-io/evcc, MIT License for the
core, some device modules are proprietary). OpenAmpere only talks to evcc's documented REST API and
contains no evcc code. Thanks to the evcc community.

Register definitions for the Weidmüller AC SMART wallbox are based on facts from Weidmüller's public document "AC
SMART ECO / VALUE / ADVANCED Modbus-Register" (2933050000 Rev. 02, 2023-06); the word order of 32-bit values on owner
reports in evcc's issues. OpenAmpere contains no evcc code.

The local protocol of Tuya devices (version 3.5, used for the portable dé wallbox) follows the facts described by
tinytuya (https://github.com/jasonacox/tinytuya, Copyright (c) 2024 Jason Cox, MIT License); OpenAmpere has its own
implementation and contains no tinytuya code. The data points of the dé wallbox are based on facts from tuya-local
(https://github.com/make-all/tuya-local, MIT License) and EV_charger (https://github.com/lachand/EV_charger,
MIT License), and on status reads an owner shared.

Daily smart-meter values of Netze BW are fetched from its customer portal meine.netze-bw.de. The endpoints and
the login flow are based on facts from ha-netze-bw (https://github.com/cygnusb/ha-netze-bw), Copyright (c) 2026
cygnusb, MIT License. The CSV format of the quarter-hour values is based on facts from a working importer a
user shared in issue #67. OpenAmpere is not affiliated with Netze BW.

The data of cars of the Volkswagen group (state of charge, range, odometer) is read from the group's EU Data Act
portal eu-data-act.drivesomethinggreater.com with the owner's login. The login flow, the client ids per brand and the
names of the data points are based on facts from evcc's vehicle/vw/eudataact (https://github.com/evcc-io/evcc, MIT
License), which is itself based on ioBroker.vw-connect (https://github.com/TA2k/ioBroker.vw-connect, MIT License);
OpenAmpere has its own implementation and contains no code of either. OpenAmpere is not affiliated with the
Volkswagen group or its brands.

my-PV heating rods are controlled through the manufacturer's Modbus TCP interface (power setpoint only).

The Home Assistant buttons on the website (site/badges/*.svg) are the badges of My Home Assistant
(https://github.com/home-assistant/my.home-assistant.io, public/badges, commit 0beba65), Copyright Home Assistant
and contributors, Apache License 2.0 (copy in site/badges/LICENSE.md, also published next to them). They are served
by the website itself, unchanged, so a visit sends nothing to my.home-assistant.io. The Home Assistant name and logo in
them belong to their owners; the buttons are used as intended, to link to My Home Assistant.

